%T A401958 588068059,588068093,588068099,588068111,588068309,588068311,
%U A401958 588068333,588068423,588068431,588068477,588068483,588068717,588069137,588069169,588069409,588069529,588069809,588070897
%N A401958 Primes p for which pi_{8,5}(p) - pi_{8,1}(p) = -1, where pi_{m,a}(x) is the number of primes <= x which are congruent to a (mod m).
%C A401958 Another version of A297448, where the primes p are not necessarily congruent to 1 modulo 4.
%C A401958 Let H be a proper subgroup of (Z/NZ)* containing all the squares, and S != H be a coset of H. Assuming the generalized Riemann hypothesis (GRH) and the Grand Simplicity Hypothesis (GSH; called LI in page 29 of Greg Martin's 2010 link), then pi_{N;S}(x) > pi_{N;H}(x) occurs more often than not: the set {pi_{N;S}(x) > pi_{N;H}(x)} has logarithmic density greater than 1/2. This phenomenon is called "Chebyshev's bias".
%C A401958 Chebyshev's bias can also be seen from the perspective of Dirichlet characters. Let T be a nontrivial coset of a nontrivial subgroup of real Dirichlet characters modulo N. Let H be the subgroup of (Z/NZ)* of elements r such that chi(r) = 1 for all r in T, and S be the coset of H consisting of elements r such that chi(r) = -1 for all r in T, then pi_{N;S}(x) > pi_{N;H}(x) occurs more often than not.
%C A401958 Here we have N = 8, H = {1} and S = {5} (equivalently, N = 24, H = {1,17} and S = {5,13}), corresponding to T = {chi_8, chi_{-8}}, where chi_d = (d/.) is the Kronecker symbol viewed as a character modulo N = 8 (or modulo N = 24).
%C A401958 This sequence gives primes p such that Sum_{primes q<=p} (chi_8(q) + chi_{-8}(q))/2 = 1.
%C A401958 Assuming GRH and LI, the set of x with pi_{8,5}(x) > pi_{8,1}(x) has logarithmic density 0.99739, making the race between {1,17} and {5,13} the 8th most biased among the 21 races of cosets of index 4 in (Z/24Z)*; see the Zenodo preprint below.
%C A401958 Let D(x) = pi_{8;5}(x) - pi_{8;1}(x). Below x0 = 7*10^12 there are respectively 30659637567, 30659688364, 30659698140, 30659716342, 30659718072, 30659718286, 30659752652, and 30659759379 primes congruent to 1, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19, or 23 modulo 24, giving D(x0) = 50583. There are 5 sign-changing zones giving a total of 396877 terms below x0:
%C A401958   (1) a(1..1014) = 588067889-593871587 (there are 20777 primes a(1) <= p <= a(1014) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -57 at p = 592258217);
%C A401958   (2) a(1015..45695) = 35615130497-37335021851 (there are 51455838 primes a(1015) <= p <= a(45695) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -1945 at p = 36983065913);
%C A401958   (3) a(45696..265027) = 5267226902633-5312932515721 (there are 1034627734 primes a(45696) <= p <= a(265027) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -6368 at p = 5281864004321);
%C A401958   (4) a(265028..360934) = 5758938230761-5768749719539 (there are 144262817 primes a(265028) <= p <= a(360934) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -3808 at p = 5760218241977);
%C A401958   (5) a(360935..396877) = 6200509945537-6209511651289 (there are 152357215 primes a(360935) <= p <= a(396877) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -4873 at p = 6203954804129).
%C A401958 The total number of primes p <= x0 with D(p) < 0 is 1382724381.
%H A401958 Jianing Song, <a href="/A401958/b401958.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..45695</a> (the first 2 sign-changing zones)
%H A401958 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24020025">Logarithmic densities of prime number races between the cosets of a subgroup of index 4 modulo 24</a>, preprint, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A401958 Greg Martin and Daniel Fiorilli, <a href="https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~gerg/slides/Chennai-31Aug10.pdf">Prime number races: An asymptotic formula for the densities</a>, Analytic and Combinatorial Number Theory, Institute of Mathematical Sciences, Chennai, India. August 31, 2010.
%H A401958 Greg Martin, <a href="https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~gerg/slides/Canberra-29Nov18.pdf">Prime number races</a>, Pure Math Seminar, UNSW Canberra. November 29, 2018.
%H A401958 Andrey S. Shchebetov, <a href="https://math101.guru/wp-content/uploads/2018/09/01-A3-Presentation-v7.3EN-no.pdf">Testing Chebyshev's bias for prime numbers up to 10^15</a>, EUCYS 2018.
%H A401958 Jianing Song, <a href="/A401958/a401958.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..396877</a> (all terms below 7*10^12, based on the Andrey S. Shchebetov link).
%H A401958 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chebyshev%27s_bias">Chebyshev's bias</a>.
%o A401958 (PARI) my(k=0); forprime(p=2, 1e11, if(p%8==1, k--); if(p%8==5, k++); if(k==-1, print1(p, ", ")))
%Y A401958 Races between cosets of index 4 in (Z/24Z)*:
